## ec-micro | Supply and demand | econ | 0 | - | ec-money
explain 2 | Supply and demand set the price
- Higher prices: buyers want less, sellers offer more.
- Where the curves cross is the equilibrium price.
- *Shortages and gluts push the price back toward it.*
> Price ceilings cause shortages

explain 3 | Price controls: good intentions, odd effects
- A cap below the market price means demand exceeds supply: queues and black markets.
- A floor above it (like some minimum prices) can create surpluses.
- *Economists argue a lot about how big these effects are in practice, especially for minimum wages.*
> Elasticity: how much demand responds

quiz 3 | Which typically has the most price-elastic demand?
* A specific brand of cereal
o Insulin
o Petrol, in the short run
o Salt
= Close substitutes make demand elastic: raise one brand's price and people switch.
= Necessities with no substitutes are inelastic.
> Opportunity cost is the real price

explain 3 | Opportunity cost
- The true cost of something is the next best thing you give up.
- A free concert ticket still costs you the evening.
- *University's biggest cost for many students is the wages they don't earn.*
> Next: game theory

## ec-game | Game theory | econ | 0 | - | ec-micro
explain 4 | Nash equilibrium
- A set of strategies where no player gains by changing theirs alone.
- John Nash proved in 1950 that every finite game has at least one (possibly with mixed strategies).
- *An equilibrium can be bad for everyone, as in the prisoner's dilemma.*
? What is a Nash equilibrium? :: A strategy profile where no player can do better by changing only their own strategy
> The ultimatum game

explain 4 | The ultimatum game
- One player splits £10 and offers some to the other. If refused, both get nothing.
- Pure self-interest says accept any offer above zero.
- *In experiments, offers below about 20–30% are often rejected: people pay to punish unfairness.*
> Chicken and brinkmanship

explain 3 | Chicken: whoever swerves loses
- Two drivers head at each other. Swerving is safe but humiliating; neither swerving is a disaster.
- Committing visibly (throwing your steering wheel out of the window) can win.
- *Cold War nuclear strategy was studied this way.*
> Auctions are games too

story 4 | The winner's curse
- In auctions for things of uncertain value, the winner is often whoever overestimated it most.
- Oil companies bidding for drilling rights in the 1960s–70s found their winning tracts often underperformed.
- *Smart bidders shade their bids down.*
> Next: behavioural economics

## ec-behav | Behavioural economics | econ | 0 | - | ec-game
explain 4 | Nudges: changing choices without banning anything
- Switching pension schemes from opt-in to opt-out raised participation dramatically in many studies.
- People stick with defaults.
- *Thaler and Sunstein's 2008 book "Nudge" popularised the idea; Thaler won the 2017 Nobel.*
> Mental accounting

explain 3 | Mental accounting
- People treat money differently depending on its label: a tax refund feels like "free money".
- Losing a £20 theatre ticket feels different from losing a £20 note, though they're equivalent.
- *Money is fungible; our minds don't treat it that way.*
> Hyperbolic discounting

explain 4 | Present bias
- Many people prefer £100 today over £110 next week, but £110 in 53 weeks over £100 in 52.
- Same one-week wait, opposite choices.
- *That inconsistency is why gym memberships go unused.*
> The endowment effect

story 4 | The mug experiment
- Students given a mug demanded about twice as much to sell it as other students would pay to buy one.
- Owning something makes it feel more valuable.
- *Kahneman, Knetsch and Thaler, 1990.*
> Next: money and central banks

## ec-macro | Money and central banks | econ | 0 | - | ec-behav
explain 3 | Most money is created by banks making loans
- When a bank lends, it creates a new deposit: new money.
- The Bank of England said so explicitly in a 2014 bulletin.
- *Physical cash is a small fraction of the money supply in the UK.*
> Inflation targets

explain 3 | Why central banks target about 2% inflation
- Zero risks tipping into deflation, which can freeze spending and make debts heavier.
- A little inflation gives room to cut real interest rates in a slump.
- *New Zealand pioneered explicit inflation targeting in 1990.*
> Hyperinflation

story 4 | Hungary 1946: prices doubled roughly every 15 hours
- At its peak, Hungary's monthly inflation reached about 4 × 10¹⁶ percent.
- The largest banknote issued was 100 quintillion pengő.
- *The worst hyperinflation on record.*
> Interest rates are the main lever

quiz 3 | A central bank raises interest rates. What is the usual intended effect?
* Slow borrowing and spending to bring inflation down
o Speed up the economy
o Increase inflation
o Weaken the currency
= Higher rates make borrowing costlier and saving more attractive.
= They usually strengthen the currency too.
> Next: bubbles

## ec-bubble | Bubbles and crashes | econ | 0 | - | ec-macro
story 4 | Tulip mania was smaller than the legend
- 1637: Dutch tulip bulb prices spiked and then collapsed.
- Historians now think the economic damage was limited and many tales were exaggerated by moralising pamphlets.
- *It's still the most famous bubble story.*
> Newton lost a fortune in the South Sea Bubble

story 4 | Newton lost a fortune in the South Sea Bubble
- 1720: shares in the South Sea Company soared, then crashed.
- Newton reportedly sold early at a profit, bought back near the top, and lost heavily.
- *Attributed quote: "I can calculate the motions of heavenly bodies, but not the madness of people."*
> The 2008 crisis

explain 4 | 2008 in one card
- US banks issued mortgages to risky borrowers, bundled them into securities rated as safe, and sold them widely.
- When house prices fell, losses spread through a highly leveraged, interconnected system.
- *Lehman Brothers' collapse on 15 September 2008 triggered a global panic.*
> Next: personal finance

## ec-pf | Personal finance maths | econ | 0 | - | ec-bubble
explain 3 | Index funds beat most professionals
- Most actively managed funds underperform their benchmark index over 10–15 years, after fees.
- Low-cost index funds just buy the whole market.
- *Warren Buffett won a 10-year, $1 million bet (2008–2017) that an S&P 500 fund would beat a set of hedge funds.*
> Fees compound too

quiz 4 | Over 40 years at 6% growth, how much does a 1% annual fee cut your final pot, roughly?
* About 30%
o About 1%
o About 10%
o About 60%
= Growth at 5% vs 6% for 40 years: 1.05⁴⁰/1.06⁴⁰ ≈ 7.04/10.29 ≈ 0.68.
= Small percentages compound into big differences.
> Starting early beats saving more

real 4 | Starting at 25 vs 35
- Saving the same monthly amount from 25 instead of 35 can mean a much larger pot at 65, purely from 10 extra years of compounding.
- At 7% growth, money roughly doubles every decade.
- *Time in the market is the biggest lever you control.*
> Economics track complete. Next: biology

## bi-evo | Evolution | bio | 0 | - | bi-life
explain 3 | Natural selection in three steps
- Variation: individuals differ. Inheritance: some differences are passed on. Selection: some variants leave more offspring.
- Over many generations, populations change.
- *No goal or foresight required.*
> Darwin sat on his idea for 20 years

story 4 | Darwin was nearly scooped
- Darwin developed natural selection by the early 1840s but didn't publish.
- 1858: Alfred Russel Wallace sent him an essay with the same idea.
- *Their papers were presented together; Darwin published On the Origin of Species in 1859.*
> Evolution observed in real time

story 4 | The peppered moth
- In industrial Britain, soot-darkened trees favoured dark moths over pale ones.
- Dark forms became dominant near cities; after clean air laws, pale moths rebounded.
- *A textbook case of natural selection, confirmed by later studies.*
> Whales evolved from land mammals

explain 4 | Whales' closest living relatives are hippos
- Fossils trace whales back about 50 million years to small, four-legged, land-dwelling ancestors.
- Some modern whales still have tiny vestigial hip bones.
- *DNA places hippos as their nearest living kin.*
> Next: genetics

## bi-gene | Genetics | bio | 0 | - | bi-evo
story 4 | Mendel's peas were ignored for 35 years
- Gregor Mendel, a monk, published his pea-breeding laws of inheritance in 1866.
- Hardly anyone noticed. Three scientists rediscovered them independently in 1900.
- *He died thinking his work had been in vain.*
> DNA's structure, 1953

story 4 | Photo 51
- Rosalind Franklin's X-ray image of DNA, "Photo 51", was shown to James Watson without her knowledge.
- Watson and Crick published the double helix in 1953.
- *Franklin died in 1958; the 1962 Nobel went to Watson, Crick and Wilkins.*
> Humans share most of their DNA with each other

quiz 3 | Roughly how similar are any two humans' DNA sequences?
* About 99.9%
o About 90%
o About 99.99999%
o About 50%
= Most human variation is in that 0.1%: millions of letters.
= Humans and chimpanzees are roughly 98–99% similar in comparable DNA.
> CRISPR edits genes like text

explain 4 | CRISPR: a bacterial immune system turned editing tool
- Bacteria store snippets of viral DNA and use Cas proteins to cut matching invaders.
- Doudna and Charpentier showed in 2012 it can be programmed to cut any chosen DNA sequence.
- *Nobel Prize in Chemistry, 2020. The first CRISPR-based therapy was approved in 2023, for sickle cell disease.*
> Next: the brain

## bi-brain | The brain | bio | 0 | - | bi-gene
myth 4 F | We only use 10% of our brains.
= Brain imaging shows activity across virtually all regions over a day.
= The brain is about 2% of body weight but uses about 20% of resting energy: evolution wouldn't keep a 90% idle organ.
> Phineas Gage

story 4 | Phineas Gage survived an iron rod through his skull
- 1848: an explosion drove a 1 m tamping iron through the railway worker's left frontal lobe.
- He survived, but friends said he was "no longer Gage": his personality changed.
- *An early clue that specific brain regions shape character and judgement.*
> Neurons and synapses

quiz 3 | Roughly how many neurons are in the human brain?
* About 86 billion
o About 1 billion
o About 1 trillion
o About 10 million
= Plus a similar number of glial cells, and an estimated 100 trillion or more synapses.
> Plasticity: the brain rewires

explain 4 | London taxi drivers grow their hippocampus
- Drivers learning "The Knowledge" of London's streets showed growth in the posterior hippocampus over training.
- The hippocampus is key for spatial memory.
- *Adult brains change with practice.* (Maguire et al., 2000s.)
> Next: microbes

## bi-mic | Microbes and medicine | bio | 0 | - | bi-brain
story 5 | Fleming's messy lab discovered penicillin
- 1928: Alexander Fleming returned from holiday to find mould had killed bacteria on a culture plate.
- Purifying it took until the 1940s, by Florey, Chain and colleagues.
- *Antibiotics have since saved hundreds of millions of lives.*
> Antibiotic resistance is evolution in fast-forward

explain 4 | Antibiotic resistance
- Bacteria divide in as little as 20 minutes: billions of generations of variation.
- Antibiotics kill the susceptible, leaving resistant strains to multiply.
- *Antimicrobial resistance was linked to over a million deaths in 2019.*
> Vaccines started with cowpox

story 4 | Edward Jenner and the milkmaids
- Milkmaids who caught mild cowpox seemed immune to deadly smallpox.
- 1796: Jenner inoculated a boy with cowpox, then exposed him to smallpox. He didn't get ill.
- *"Vaccine" comes from vacca, Latin for cow. Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980.*
> Viruses aren't quite alive

explain 4 | Are viruses alive?
- Viruses can't reproduce or make energy on their own; they hijack cells.
- Giant viruses like mimivirus have more genes than some bacteria.
- *They sit on the blurry edge of what counts as life.*
> Next: animal minds

## bi-anim | Animal intelligence | bio | 0 | - | bi-mic
story 4 | Crows solve multi-step puzzles
- New Caledonian crows make and use hook tools from twigs.
- In experiments, they've solved puzzles needing several steps in the right order.
- *Some crows recognise individual human faces and hold grudges.*
> Elephants and the mirror test

explain 3 | The mirror test
- Mark an animal's body where it can only see it in a mirror. Does it touch the mark on itself?
- Great apes, dolphins, elephants and magpies have passed versions of it.
- *Cleaner wrasse fish passed in a 2019 study, provoking debate about what the test measures.*
> Bees count and communicate

explain 4 | The waggle dance
- Honeybees communicate the direction and distance of flowers by dancing.
- The angle of the waggle run relative to vertical encodes direction relative to the sun.
- *Karl von Frisch decoded it and shared the 1973 Nobel Prize.*
> Octopuses escape tanks

story 3 | Inky the octopus escaped to the sea
- 2016: an octopus at New Zealand's National Aquarium squeezed out of a gap in his tank.
- He apparently slid across the floor and down a 50 m drainpipe into the ocean.
- *Octopuses can squeeze through any gap larger than their beak.*
> Biology track complete
